          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been anxious for months and now I am not sleeping. How is anxiety assessed, what does guidance describe as first-line, and is the insomnia treated separately?",
          "answer": "Nothing in your description matched the emergency patterns the tool checks for. That is not a guarantee that you are safe. If your anxiety or sleep gets worse, or you have thoughts of harming yourself, seek medical help promptly.\n\n**Insomnia:** The retrieved guidance on chronic insomnia (sleep medicine and internal medicine society guidance, document \"guideline-insomnia-cbt-first-line\") describes cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) as the first-line treatment for adults, ahead of medication. It is delivered over roughly four to eight sessions and includes steps such as using the bed only for sleep and limiting time in bed. The same guidance notes that its effects tend to last after treatment ends, and that it avoids the risks of long-term sleep medicines. It also suggests reviewing caffeine, alcohol and nicotine, since alcohol can make sleep worse later in the night.\n\n**Anxiety assessment and first-line treatment:** The results I received did not cover this. They contained no guidance on how anxiety is assessed or which anxiety treatments come first. I cannot answer those parts from the retrieved material, and I won't fill the gap from memory. A clinician can explain how anxiety is assessed and what options fit your situation.\n\n**Is the insomnia treated separately?** The insomnia guidance describes CBT-I as a treatment for insomnia in its own right. It does not say whether insomnia that occurs alongside anxiety is handled differently, so I can't tell you from these sources. This is a good question to bring to a clinician, who can look at both together.\n\nA reasonable next step is to book an appointment and say what you told me: months of anxiety, now poor sleep. Ask how both will be assessed and whether CBT-I is an option for you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
